# OSWA Content Review — Batch 1 "Core Acquisition"
10 July 2026 · 5 pages reviewed against their briefs, current Google results, and Australian health-advertising rules (AHPRA/TGA/MBS — item numbers checked against MBS Online).

**How to read this pack:** start here, then open the per-page file for whichever page you're working on — `gastric-sleeve-perth.md`, `gastric-bypass-perth.md`, `bariatric-surgery-perth.md`, `weight-loss-surgery-perth.md`, `am-i-eligible.md`. Every issue in those files comes with the exact draft quote and a paste-ready rewrite. Fresh keyword/SERP data is in `../ahrefs-refresh-2026-07-10.md`. (`_worklog/` is internal working notes — ignore.)

## Verdicts

| Page | Verdict | Must-fix | Should-fix | Why it can't ship yet (one line) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Gastric Sleeve Perth | **Rework** | 7 | 11 | "Safe, powerful, and lasting" + "life-changing" claims, quoted $4,000 fee, absolute "will cover" insurance wording |
| Gastric Bypass Perth | **Rework** | 5 | 22 | Medicare item 31581 wrongly applied to Roux-en-Y, $6,000 fee + literal "$[X,XXX]" placeholder, CTA says "gastric sleeve" |
| Bariatric Surgery Perth (hub) | **Rework** | 5 | 12 | Semaglutide + GLP-1 mentions (TGA), "make bariatric procedures safe", coverage certainty |
| Weight Loss Surgery Perth | **Rework** | 3 | 10 | GLP-1 "suitable if BMI 30+" overclaim, unsupported "consistently achieve better outcomes" — **lightest fix in the batch** |
| Am I Eligible | **Rework** | 5 | 12 | "Almost everyone with BMI 40+ is eligible", "never a reason to be turned away", finance content crept back in |

All verdicts are about fixable copy, not structure — the writer's structure, tone and empathy are genuinely good, and nothing needs a restart. **Importantly, a chunk of the flagged wording was inherited from our own briefs** (detail below), so this is a joint fix pass across drafts AND briefs, not a critique of the writer.

## The 5 themes (fix once, apply everywhere)

**1. AHPRA claim balance (must-fix).** Unbalanced claims on every page: "safe, powerful, and lasting", "life-changing", "generally safe", outcome certainty ("your appetite will also be reduced", "consistently achieve better outcomes"). Each per-page file has paste-ready rewrites. **Brief-inherited subset:** the hub's "make these procedures safe for most suitable candidates" and the bypass keyhole "less pain" line implement the briefs near-verbatim — those brief sections need the same correction.

**2. TGA prescription-medicine references (must-fix + a policy decision).** Not just one semaglutide mention: the hub names semaglutide and, with the eligibility page, carries several GLP-1 *class* references. TGA guidance captures direct **and indirect** references to prescription-medicine classes (GLP-1s, "weight-loss injections") in public health-service advertising. The briefs themselves mandate "GLP-1 medical weight management" coverage, so this is a site-wide question — how does the practice want to describe medical weight management? — to settle once (with legal input) and apply to briefs and drafts together.

**3. Medicare/BMI accuracy + cost certainty (must-fix).** Drafts state "BMI 35+, or 30–35 with conditions" next to MBS item numbers; the MBS wording (explanatory note TN.8.29) is BMI 40+, or 35+ with major comorbidities — lower-BMI pathways must read "may be considered for assessment", never "you may qualify". **Brief-inherited:** the sleeve and bypass briefs mandate the 35/30–35 framing — correct the briefs too. Also: item 31581 (BPD/duodenal switch) cited on the Roux-en-Y page (should be 31572 only); quoted fees $4,000/$6,000 plus a literal "$[X,XXX]" placeholder against the briefs' no-single-figure rule; and absolute insurance claims ("gold-tier… covers/will cover the hospital and theatre component") need policy-, waiting-period- and gap-aware wording.

**4. Leftover working notes in the copy (must-fix, trivial).** "Conversion CTA headline" labels, inline "Alt" headline options, an "Option A" heading, planning notes — and the bypass CTA that says *gastric sleeve*. Must be resolved before build. (The brief + keyword table at the top of each doc is fine — it's clearly reference material, not page copy, and isn't counted against anyone.)

**5. Length + FAQ coverage (should-fix).** Body-only counts incl. FAQs (FAQs count toward the target, as agreed 1 Jul): 1,844 / 1,783 / 2,091 / 1,958 / 1,908 vs targets of 1,000–1,500 — roughly 280–900 over, and the writer's own notes show she aimed at ~1,740–2,000. Decide once: enforce the brief targets (per-page cut lists are in the reviews) or consciously accept ~1,800. While cutting, add the missing FAQ topics Google is currently asking (cost *in Perth*, the 30/30 eating rule, who is *not* eligible, Medicare coverage, 10-year outcomes).

## Two questions for Marcus/client

1. **Which hospitals are current?** The briefs say St John of God Murdoch/Subiaco; all five drafts consistently say Mount Private, The Park Private and Waikiki Private, with consults at Garden City Booragoon + WA Cardiology Greenfields (Greenfields = the Mandurah suburb, so that part is just naming granularity). The drafts' consistency suggests the writer has newer client info. **Nobody rewrites either direction until this is confirmed** — whichever source is wrong gets corrected.
2. **GLP-1 policy** (theme 2 above): how the practice wants to reference medical weight management publicly — a TGA/legal call that affects every page and the briefs.

## Also noted
- Positive: hub vs spokes carve-up is mostly right (no competing H1s); the bypass-vs-sleeve angle is well handled; tone matches the "sceptical of promises, responds to honesty" reader.
- Sleeve/bypass pages share verbatim benefit/risk lead sentences — vary per procedure.
- Keyword data movement since the June briefs: "gastric sleeve perth" difficulty is now 36 (brief said 7 — changes expectation-setting, not the content); "gastric bypass perth" (36/mo) now outweighs the brief's primary "gastric bypass surgery perth" (18/mo) — lead with the shorter form; everything else within normal drift.
- E-E-A-T: no named surgeons/reviewer line on any page — add "Medically reviewed by [Dr, FRACS]" + citations (WA Health, MBS items).

## Suggested WhatsApp message for Marcus

> oswa review done ✅ all 5 core pages reviewed against the briefs + fresh SERP data. honest verdict: the writing itself is genuinely good — right structure, right tone — but none are publishable yet. main issues: AHPRA/TGA compliance (unbalanced "safe"/"life-changing" claims, GLP-1/semaglutide mentions — TGA restricts naming prescription med classes in public advertising), Medicare/BMI wording that overpromises vs the actual MBS criteria, single dollar fees quoted where the briefs said not to, and leftover working notes (one CTA on the bypass page literally says gastric sleeve 😅). important context for the writer: a fair chunk of the flagged wording (the BMI framing, the "safe for most candidates" line, the GLP-1 coverage) came from OUR briefs — she followed them. so this is a fix pass on drafts + briefs together, and every issue has a paste-ready rewrite in the review docs. two things i need from you/client: (1) are surgeries at SJOG murdoch/subiaco (briefs) or mount private/the park/waikiki (all 5 drafts say this — she may have newer info)? (2) how does the practice want to handle mentioning GLP-1/medical weight management publicly — that's a TGA/legal call that affects every page. sending the review pack now.

