| Gastric Bypass Perth | **Rework** | 5 | 22 | Medicare item 31581 wrongly applied to Roux-en-Y, quoted fee + a literal "$[X,XXX]" placeholder, CTA says "gastric sleeve" |
| Bariatric Surgery Perth (hub) | **Rework** | 5 | 12 | Semaglutide + GLP-1 mentions (TGA), "make bariatric procedures safe", coverage certainty |
| Weight Loss Surgery Perth | **Rework** | 3 | 10 | GLP-1 "suitable if BMI 30+" overclaim, unsupported outcomes claim — **lightest fix in the batch** |
| Am I Eligible | **Rework** | 5 | 12 | "Almost everyone with BMI 40+ is eligible", "never a reason to be turned away", finance content crept back in |

All verdicts are about fixable copy, not structure — the writing's structure, tone and empathy are genuinely good, and nothing needs a restart. **Importantly, a chunk of the flagged wording was inherited from the briefs themselves** (detail below), so this is a joint fix pass across drafts AND briefs, not a critique of the writer.

## The 5 themes (fix once, apply everywhere)

**1. AHPRA claim balance (must-fix).** Unbalanced claims on every page: "safe, powerful, and lasting", "life-changing", "generally safe", outcome certainty ("your appetite will also be reduced", "consistently achieve better outcomes"). Each per-page review has paste-ready rewrites. **Brief-inherited subset:** the hub's "make these procedures safe for most suitable candidates" and the bypass keyhole "less pain" line implement the briefs near-verbatim — those brief sections need the same correction.

**2. TGA prescription-medicine references (must-fix + a policy decision).** Not just one semaglutide mention: the hub names semaglutide and, with the eligibility page, carries several GLP-1 *class* references. TGA guidance captures direct **and indirect** references to prescription-medicine classes (GLP-1s, "weight-loss injections") in public health-service advertising. The briefs themselves mandate "GLP-1 medical weight management" coverage, so this is a site-wide question — how does the practice want to describe medical weight management? — to settle once (with legal input) and apply to briefs and drafts together.

**3. Medicare/BMI accuracy + cost certainty (must-fix).** Drafts state "BMI 35+, or 30–35 with conditions" next to MBS item numbers; the MBS wording (explanatory note TN.8.29) is BMI 40+, or 35+ with major comorbidities — lower-BMI pathways must read "may be considered for assessment", never "you may qualify". **Brief-inherited:** the sleeve and bypass briefs mandate the 35/30–35 framing — correct the briefs too. Also: item 31581 (BPD/duodenal switch) cited on the Roux-en-Y page (should be 31572 only); single dollar fees quoted against the briefs' no-single-figure rule, plus a literal "$[X,XXX]" placeholder; and absolute insurance claims ("gold-tier… covers/will cover the hospital and theatre component") need policy-, waiting-period- and gap-aware wording.

**4. Leftover working notes in the copy (must-fix, trivial).** "Conversion CTA headline" labels, inline "Alt" headline options, an "Option A" heading, planning notes — and the bypass CTA that says *gastric sleeve*. Must be resolved before build. (The brief + keyword table at the top of each doc is fine — it's clearly reference material, not page copy, and isn't counted against anyone.)

**5. Length + FAQ coverage (should-fix).** Body-only counts incl. FAQs (FAQs count toward the target, as agreed 1 Jul): 1,844 / 1,783 / 2,091 / 1,958 / 1,908 vs targets of 1,000–1,500 — roughly 280–900 over. Decide once: enforce the brief targets (per-page cut lists are in the reviews) or consciously accept ~1,800. While cutting, add the missing FAQ topics Google is currently asking (cost *in Perth*, the 30/30 eating rule, who is *not* eligible, Medicare coverage, 10-year outcomes).

## Two questions to settle before anyone edits

1. **Which hospitals are current?** The briefs say St John of God Murdoch/Subiaco; all five drafts consistently say Mount Private, The Park Private and Waikiki Private, with consults at Garden City Booragoon + WA Cardiology Greenfields (Greenfields = the Mandurah suburb, so that part is just naming granularity). The drafts' consistency suggests the writer has newer information. **Nobody rewrites either direction until this is confirmed** — whichever source is wrong gets corrected.
2. **GLP-1 policy** (theme 2 above): how the practice wants to reference medical weight management publicly — a TGA/legal call that affects every page and the briefs.

## Also noted

- Positive: hub vs spokes carve-up is mostly right (no competing H1s); the bypass-vs-sleeve angle is well handled; tone matches the "sceptical of promises, responds to honesty" reader.
- Sleeve/bypass pages share verbatim benefit/risk lead sentences — vary per procedure.
- Keyword data movement since the June briefs: "gastric sleeve perth" difficulty is now 36 (brief said 7 — changes expectation-setting, not the content); "gastric bypass perth" (36/mo) now outweighs the brief's primary "gastric bypass surgery perth" (18/mo) — lead with the shorter form; everything else within normal drift.
- E-E-A-T: no named surgeons/reviewer line on any page — add "Medically reviewed by [Dr, FRACS]" + citations (WA Health, MBS items).

---

## Update — gap-closing second pass (12 Jul 2026)

The first review fixed the first instance of each problem; this pass made it exhaustive. Per page: every residual flagged claim now has its own paste-ready quote + replacement (insurance wording repeated in FAQs, leftover safety/effectiveness phrasing, secondary GLP-1 mentions); all BMI/eligibility lines use the MBS-accurate framing (BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major comorbidity, always assessment-based); cut-lists are now full quoted passages with exact replacements; the missing high-demand FAQ answers (cost in Perth, 30/30 rule, who is not eligible, Medicare, 10-year outcomes) are written AHPRA-safe; internal links point at real destination pages; scaffolding artifacts are all flagged; and each page's brief has been corrected so the flagged wording doesn't recur. Per-page must/should-fix counts have grown accordingly — see each page's review.

Two independent compliance review passes were run over this rework; all blocking findings applied. Quantified outcome stats (e.g. 60–70% / 70–80% excess-weight loss) are retained only as verify-gated notes, never as paste-ready copy.

Still open (blocking final publication, not this review): hospital identity (which facilities the practice operates at) and the GLP-1/medical-weight-management public-advertising policy — interim rule is class/ingredient names only, no brand names.

