content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:307:- **Delete the extraction instruction:** “Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Orient the reader to what they will find next — a plain overview of the four main surgical options so they can walk into their consultation with a sense of what the conversation will cover.” Replace it with: “Below is a plain overview of the main surgical and non-surgical pathways, with links to more detailed procedure pages if you want to compare options before your consultation.”
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:316:  > **Procedure day** Obesity Surgery WA’s bariatric surgery is performed via keyhole surgery in most cases, with short post-operative hospital stays (1-3 nights) at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Guided by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll start on fluids and your recovery will be monitored until you’re ready to return home.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:328:  > **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:336:  > **Medical weight management**: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a surgical procedure.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:349:  > Every surgical procedure carries risk, though modern keyhole techniques and highly experienced surgeons make bariatric procedures safe for most suitable candidates. Below are some of the most common risks, potential complications, and side effects of undergoing bariatric surgery:
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:375:  > **1. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?** The right procedure depends on your health history, weight loss goals, existing health conditions (like reflux or diabetes), and your lifestyle. There is no universal answer; what works well for one person may not be the best fit for another. This is exactly what your initial consultation is for. Your surgeon will review your circumstances thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe, sustained results for you specifically.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:377:  > **2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or private health insurance?** Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:379:  > **3. What is the difference between a gastric sleeve and a gastric bypass?** A sleeve gastrectomy reduces the size of the stomach, leaving digestion otherwise unchanged. A gastric bypass does the same but also reroutes the small intestine, meaning the body absorbs fewer kilojoules from food. The bypass tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and stronger outcomes for type 2 diabetes and reflux, but is a more complex procedure with greater long-term nutritional demands. Your surgeon will help determine which is the better procedure for your situation.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:381:  > **4. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?** Surgery is not the only path. Medical weight management, including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs, is a genuine and effective option for many people, and our team offers this alongside surgical pathways. Some patients pursue medical management first and decide to progress to surgery later; others find it sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, not a particular procedure.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:385:  > **6. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?** Consultations take place at our clinics in Booragoon or Greenfields, with surgeries performed at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Once your surgery is planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises on the day.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:407:> Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but eligibility is assessed individually. Verify the planned item number: 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch. Private-health benefits depend on the fund, policy, waiting periods and exclusions.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:411:> The choice depends on your medical history, eating patterns, health conditions, goals and willingness to follow the required aftercare. Your surgeon should explain likely benefits, limits, risks and monitoring needs for each option; no procedure or outcome is guaranteed.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:421:> **6. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?**
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:423:> The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:427:> No online checklist can decide suitability. BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold; other clinical or practical factors may affect suitability, including surgical risk, unresolved medical issues and ability to complete required follow-up. Your surgeon makes an individual assessment.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:449:> Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Orient the reader to what they will find next — a plain overview of the four main surgical options so they can walk into their consultation with a sense of what the conversation will cover.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:469:- Describes laparoscopic techniques without saying they “make these procedures safe for most suitable candidates,” and prohibits comparative safety, pain or recovery claims.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:470:- States the MBS rule as BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment; BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:473:- Includes Perth cost, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not meet MBS or clinical suitability requirements, 10-year variation and how to assess a surgeon in the FAQ plan.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:474:- Marks the GLP-1/active-ingredient wording as OPEN — pending TGA/legal decision; if retained, use active-ingredient/class names only and no brand names.
content-reviews/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:476:Verification flags: hospital identity and affiliations remain to be verified; the GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy remains pending; confirm current MBS, referral and insurer details before publication.
