docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:3:Reason: Safety, Medicare eligibility, hospital verification, cost certainty and outcome-claim issues need correction before publication.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:9:1. **AHPRA risk: the opening claim overstates safety and effectiveness**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:11:> Yet in the right hands, gastric sleeve surgery is a safe, powerful, and lasting weight loss tool.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:15:Why it matters: "Safe, powerful, and lasting" creates an unbalanced beneficial expectation for a regulated health service, so risk balance needs to sit in the same sentence.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:17:2. **MBS/BMI eligibility is not accurate enough for Medicare item 31575 [also fix the brief]**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:21:> For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:23:Why it matters: The draft's BMI 30-35 framing may be a broader clinical eligibility statement, but it should not sit unqualified on a page that also discusses Medicare item 31575; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:27:> You'll spend 1-2 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:35:> Draft L228-L246: **How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:37:> Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. Medicare item number 31575 does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:39:> Total out-of-pocket costs vary depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges, complexity and any applicable gap. Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when MBS requirements are met, while private health insurance may contribute to hospital and theatre costs depending on the policy, waiting periods and fund rules. Current estimates and the components that apply to your circumstances are provided at consultation; ask the team to explain available funding pathways and review the current gastric sleeve cost guide before deciding whether to proceed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:45:> Draft L57-L61: Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:49:Why it matters: "Will also be reduced" reads like an outcome guarantee, while the rewrite keeps the mechanism and qualifies individual variation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:51:6. **The "life-changing" phrase creates a transformational-benefit claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:53:> While it can be life-changing, weight loss surgery is a tool, not a one-off fix.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:57:Why it matters: "Life-changing" risks creating an unreasonable expectation of benefit under health advertising rules, even when the surrounding sentence is otherwise balanced.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:61:> Draft L307-L315: **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:63:> **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when the MBS requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every fee. Hospital, theatre, surgeon and anaesthetist costs, private-health benefits and out-of-pocket gaps depend on your circumstances and policy, so confirm current details with your surgeon’s office and insurer. A consultation can help you understand which costs and rebates apply to you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:65:Why it matters: The cost-section replacement covers the earlier insurance claim at draft L236; this later FAQ repeats the absolute claim at L313. Coverage depends on fund rules, policy settings, waiting periods and gap arrangements, so the copy should not imply automatic cover.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:71:> Draft L54-L55: It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:75:> Draft L173-L175: Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:77:> Weight loss outcomes vary between individuals; no specific result or timeline can be guaranteed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:81:> Draft L177-L179: Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:85:> Draft L189-L191: Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:89:> Draft L199-L199: Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:93:> Draft L201-L203: Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:95:> Staple line leak is a serious potential complication that may require further treatment; reported rates vary by patient and clinical setting, so ask your surgeon how this risk is monitored and managed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:97:> Draft L205-L207: Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:101:> Draft L214-L217: Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:105:> Draft L283-L289: **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:109:> Draft L300-L305: **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon, as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:113:Why it matters: These phrases imply guaranteed benefit, minimise risk, or make comparative safety/effectiveness claims. The replacements keep the information useful while making outcomes and recovery individual and measured.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:131:Why it matters: The draft is 1,844 words including FAQs. Use this cut-list: (1) delete the duplicate gastric-band paragraph, quoted at draft L80-L86, because the sleeve-vs-bypass comparison is already present; (2) delete the standalone "Single permanent procedure; no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band" benefit at draft L193-L195 because permanence is covered in the definition and FAQ; (3) after the cost rewrite above, remove the detailed superannuation examples and named-finance sentence at draft L240-L244 and retain one consultation-based funding sentence; and (4) keep each FAQ answer to 3–5 sentences while preserving the new cost, Medicare, 30/30 and long-term-outcomes answers below.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:135:> Draft L80-L86: Another key difference between the gastric sleeve and gastric band is that the sleeve restricts only the amount of food you can consume, not the types of foods (as is the case with the gastric band procedure).
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:139:> Draft L193-L195: Single permanent procedure; no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:143:> Draft L240-L244: For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:151:> A gastric sleeve permanently reduces stomach size, a gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, and prescription weight-management medicines, including GLP-1 medicines, work differently again; there is no single best option for everyone, and your surgeon can explain how reflux, BMI, diabetes, medication history and surgical risk affect the choice.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:159:> BMI is only a starting point. For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Your surgeon will assess broader clinical risk, nutritional needs and ability to commit to aftercare; some conditions may need management first, and some people may not be suitable for surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:163:5. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask about the 30/30 eating rule**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:167:> **5. What does recovery actually look like week to week?** The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:169:Why it matters: This writes the missing 30/30 answer into the recovery FAQ without presenting it as a universal rule; the dietitian’s plan remains authoritative.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:175:> **2. How much weight will I actually lose, and what can happen 10 years after surgery?** Weight loss and long-term changes vary between individuals. Nutrition, reflux, medical conditions, eating patterns, activity and follow-up can all matter, and ongoing clinical review and blood tests help identify issues and guide care. No specific result or timeline can be guaranteed.
