content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:33:bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band, lap band, obesity as a medical condition, BMI, GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support, type 2 diabetes, Medicare rebate, private health cover, endoscopic option, weight regain, Booragoon, Mandurah
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:48:Every top result is a surgeon homepage that funnels straight to surgery — none present medical management (GLP-1 medication, VLCD, dietitian support) as an equal pathway. That is OSWA's biggest differentiation opportunity and aligns exactly with the brief's mandate to frame obesity as a medical condition and not over-index on surgery. Second gap: the PAA boxes show high demand for cost, Medicare coverage, and BMI/eligibility answers, yet competitor homepages bury or omit these — directly addressing how medical management is assessed from BMI 27+, how Medicare rebates and MBS thresholds work, and how much treatment may cost / whether superannuation access can be explored in plain language (the brief already mandates an eligibility section, cost paragraph, and FAQs) will capture featured-snippet and PAA real estate competitors leave open. Third gap: low-DR competitors mean strong topical depth, empathetic non-judgemental framing, and a team-based (dedicated APD dietitian team, physicians + surgeons, 2 clinics) angle can outrank them without heavy backlinks. Note OSWA (obesitysurgerywa.com.au) currently sits at position 5 with DR 11, so this page is an existing-asset improvement play, not a cold start.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:76:- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Give the reader a quick mental model of the spectrum: medical management (GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support) at one end, surgical procedures (sleeve, bypass, band) at the other. Acknowledge that many people move between them over time, and there is no single correct starting point.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:79:- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:82:  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:111:  - GLP-1 medications can cause nausea, digestive symptoms and other side effects; not suitable for everyone
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:136:9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:144:- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your clinician will advise", "many people experience"
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:145:- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:146:- MBS surgical threshold: state BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity as assessment guidance; BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Do not present a lower-BMI statement as surgical eligibility.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:147:- GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy is pending: use active-ingredient/class names only (semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1), never brand names, and verify the final TGA/legal position before publication
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:7:> **Repositioned per client (June 2026):** medical eligibility, moved out of the finance cluster. "Am I eligible?" is a CLINICAL question (BMI, comorbidities, prior attempts, individual assessment), not a financial/affordability one — frame it medically throughout. Keep internal links cost → eligibility → book-consultation intact.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:37:bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass, BMI (body mass index), class 2 obesity, class 3 obesity, comorbidities, type 2 diabetes, obstructive sleep apnoea, high blood pressure, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD), FRACS surgeon, GP referral, Medicare rebate, private health insurance, superannuation early release, dietitian, psychological assessment, orientation consultation, St John of God Murdoch, Subiaco, metabolic surgery
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:52:Clear opportunity for a Perth-specific, reassurance-led eligibility page. Gaps: (1) The clinic pages that rank are generic BMI tick-box checklists; almost none address the emotional barrier (shame, fear of being turned away, failed diets) — the brief's empathetic angle is genuinely differentiated. (2) No top result clearly explains Australian Medicare/MBS rebates, private health cover, or superannuation early release in plain language tied to eligibility — own the 'GP referral vs self-referral' and funding angle. (3) PAA shows strong demand for 'who is NOT eligible' and 'minimum weight / BMI around 30' — directly answer the low-BMI (30–34 with comorbidity) and mental-health questions the competitors gloss over. (4) Almost no AU competitor names a specific Perth hospital/surgeon credential (SJOG Murdoch/Subiaco, FRACS) — local trust signals are a fast win. (5) Healthdirect/Mayo win on authority but are non-local and offer no booking path — capture the bottom-funnel 'now what do I do in Perth' intent they cannot serve. Match the PAA questions in FAQ schema to compete for those featured-snippet slots.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:68:| BMI 35+ | Assessed individually | BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. |
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:77:- Para 3: Give a concrete example of two people with the same BMI getting different recommendations — one with type 2 diabetes and sleep apnoea versus one with no comorbidities. This shows why in-person assessment matters.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:81:- **Frame this as CLINICAL eligibility, not financial.** The section centres on medical indicators: BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment; previous attempts at non-surgical weight loss, age and general health considerations also form part of assessment. Cost, finance and affordability are NOT eligibility criteria — keep them out of this section (they belong in the Cost paragraph and the Medicare/finance page).
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:83:- BMI summary line to anchor the section: "BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your full health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways."
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:86:  - BMI 30–34 with a weight-related condition: does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; the surgeon may discuss other clinical pathways after individual assessment
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:87:  - BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:88:  - BMI 40 or above: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:89:  - Comorbidities relevant to assessment: a major obesity-related comorbidity matters to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create eligibility below BMI 35. Whether a particular condition counts as a major obesity-related comorbidity is assessed individually against the current MBS criteria
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:107:4. Access to FRACS-certified surgeons at SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question: drafts name different facilities)
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:120:Cover: costs and any Medicare or private-health benefits depend on the procedure, policy and individual circumstances; discuss them at the first consultation and ask Medicare and the insurer to confirm what applies. The team provides a clear itemised estimate. Do not quote specific dollar figures or promise that a particular policy will cover a component.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:123:Direction: Acknowledge the emotional weight of the first step (making contact feels hard). The line should be warm and direct — it should make the reader feel that picking up the phone is safe, not a commitment.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:140:- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your surgeon will advise"
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:141:- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:142:- **AHPRA s133 (eligibility-specific):** State all eligibility criteria as GENERAL INDICATORS subject to individual surgeon assessment — never "if your BMI is X you qualify" or any self-qualifying language. Do NOT pair eligibility with guaranteed outcomes or specific weight-loss / comorbidity-resolution figures (e.g. no "resolve your diabetes", no "lose X kg"). Reference non-surgical options and multidisciplinary (MDT) assessment as part of the pathway — do not push surgery as the default. Include a clear "general information, not medical advice — individual assessment required" line on the page.
content-briefs/am-i-eligible.md:143:- **GLP-1 / medical-weight-management policy:** TGA/legal review remains pending. Prefer the neutral phrase "non-surgical medical weight management" in public copy; if an active ingredient or medication class is retained after the decision, use only semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1 wording, never brand names, and flag the copy for verification.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:5:**Why:** Safety, Medicare eligibility, hospital verification, cost certainty and outcome-claim issues need correction before publication.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:11:1. **AHPRA risk: the opening claim overstates safety and effectiveness**
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:13:> Yet in the right hands, gastric sleeve surgery is a safe, powerful, and lasting weight loss tool.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:17:Why it matters: "Safe, powerful, and lasting" creates an unbalanced beneficial expectation for a regulated health service, so risk balance needs to sit in the same sentence.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:19:2. **MBS/BMI eligibility is not accurate enough for Medicare item 31575 [also fix the brief]**
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:23:> For Medicare item 31575, MBS eligibility generally requires a BMI of 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:25:Why it matters: The draft's BMI 30-35 framing may be a broader clinical eligibility statement, but it should not sit unqualified on a page that also discusses Medicare item 31575; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:29:> You'll spend 1-2 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:39:> Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your situation. Medicare item number 31575 does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical fees can be found here.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:41:> Total out-of-pocket costs vary depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges, complexity and any applicable gap. Medicare item 31575 may attract a rebate when MBS requirements are met, while private health insurance may contribute to hospital and theatre costs depending on the policy, waiting periods and fund rules. Current estimates and the components that apply to your circumstances are provided at consultation; ask the team to explain available funding pathways and review the current gastric sleeve cost guide before deciding whether to proceed.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:51:Why it matters: "Will also be reduced" reads like an outcome guarantee, while the rewrite keeps the mechanism and qualifies individual variation.
