    11	1. **The BMI 40+ line crosses from assessment criteria into apparent self-qualification**
    12	
    13	> BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available.
    14	
    15	> Use the canonical BMI replacement in G2 #4 below.
    16	
    17	Why it matters: BMI alone should not imply eligibility for surgery, and Australian guidance should be framed as criteria used in assessment.
    18	
    19	2. **The mental-health wording is too absolute**
    20	
    21	> Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process, but it is never a reason to be turned away.
    22	
    23	> Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process. A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery, but your surgeon may recommend extra support, further review, or a different timing if that is safer for you.
    24	
    25	Why it matters: Active or unstable mental health concerns, eating disorders, substance misuse or inability to follow post-operative care can delay or change suitability, so "never" overpromises.
    26	
    27	3. **The preparation step makes an unbalanced "safe and right" claim**
    28	
    29	> This stage confirms that surgery is safe and right for you, and prepares you for the changes ahead.
    30	
    31	> This stage helps your surgeon assess whether surgery is appropriate for you, identify and explain the risks, and prepare you for the changes ahead.
    32	
    33	Why it matters: No surgery should be described as simply "safe", especially on a page that also acknowledges risks.
