    77	- BMI summary to anchor the section: "BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment."
    78	- Write each of the following points clearly — do not combine or dilute them:
    79	  - BMI 40 or above: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility
    80	  - BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment
    81	  - BMI below 35: this does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; discuss any non-MBS clinical options with the surgeon, who will assess suitability individually
    82	  - History of genuine attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise or medical means without lasting success: this is expected and not held against the patient
    83	  - Commitment to long-term dietary changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up: the surgery is one part; aftercare supports monitoring and ongoing care
    84	  - Final eligibility: always determined after a full assessment with the surgeon and wider team, including medical history review and review of conditions that affect surgical risk
    85	
    86	### How it works (6 steps)
    87	Keep steps in this order — 1–2 sentences covering the content described:
    88	
    89	1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral** — most patients start with a GP referral (helps with Medicare and private health); patients can also come directly and the team will guide them on the referral
    90	2. **Consultation with your surgeon** — discuss history, goals and health; the surgeon explains which procedure or medical pathway may be clinically appropriate, including limits, risks and follow-up
    91	3. **Assessment with the wider team** — may include physician, dietitian and other clinicians who assess suitability, risks and preparation needs
    92	4. **Pre-surgery preparation** — may include a pre-operative diet; its purpose, risks and instructions are explained individually alongside practical guidance on the planned procedure and early recovery
    93	5. **Your procedure at SJOG Murdoch or Subiaco (hospital identity to be verified — open question)** — laparoscopic in most cases; hospital stay and recovery guidance are tailored to the procedure
    94	6. **Long-term aftercare** — patient stays connected to the dietitian team and clinical staff over months and years; regular reviews, nutritional monitoring and support continue as the body and habits adjust
    95	
    96	### Procedures section (written as benefit bullets)
    97	This section replaces the generic "benefits" format — each bullet introduces one procedure. Cover all six:
    98	
    99	1. **Gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy)** — removes part of the stomach to create a smaller stomach; expected effects and suitability vary, so the surgeon explains the individual risks, benefits and follow-up needs
   100	2. **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)** — changes stomach size and how food passes through the small intestine; it has procedure-specific nutritional monitoring and risks to discuss with the surgeon
   101	3. **Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass)** — a bypass variation with a single join; the surgeon explains its technical features, risks and whether it suits the individual situation
   102	4. **Gastric band (adjustable band)** — an implanted adjustable band that can be adjusted or removed, but still requires surgery, ongoing reviews and monitoring for band-related complications
   103	5. **Revision surgery** — for patients who had a previous procedure (e.g. older band) and need conversion, repair or a change to a different procedure; risks and follow-up differ by case and are assessed individually
   104	6. **Medical weight management** — a non-surgical pathway supported by medical assessment and structured dietitian-led care; suitability and any medication or active-ingredient/class reference are OPEN — pending TGA/legal decision. If retained, use class/ingredient names only and no brands
   105	
   106	### Risks — honest section
   107	- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge every operation carries risk. Explain that laparoscopic techniques use small incisions, without making comparative safety, pain or recovery claims. Commit to giving the reader a frank picture rather than something they find out later.
   108	- Risk bullets to address (one sentence each):
   109	  - General surgical risks: bleeding, infection, blood clots, anaesthetic reactions
   110	  - Procedure-specific risks: leaks at staple or join lines in sleeve and bypass; band slippage and erosion with gastric bands
   111	  - Nutritional: bypass procedures in particular can affect absorption of vitamins and minerals — lifelong supplements and monitoring are part of the deal
   112	  - Eating changes require real adjustment: smaller portions, slower eating, avoiding certain foods — especially in the early months
   113	  - Lifestyle change is genuinely required: surgery does not remove the need for healthy habits; weight regain remains possible and follow-up needs vary
   114	  - The possibility of revision surgery depending on the procedure and how the body responds
