content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-70-- ~3 paragraphs
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-71-- Para 1: Define bariatric surgery in plain language — procedures that change how the stomach (and sometimes small intestine) handles food; goal is earlier satiety, less hunger, and in some procedures changed absorption. Contextualise it as a medical intervention for severe obesity when diet and exercise have not been enough.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:72:- Para 2: Be honest about what surgery is not — not a quick fix, not something that works alone. Acknowledge that obesity is a chronic medical condition, not a willpower problem. Surgery treats the medical condition. Long-term results depend on what the patient does alongside the procedure.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-73-- Para 3: Explain that most procedures are performed laparoscopically (keyhole), using small incisions. Do not make comparative pain, recovery or safety claims; explain that the surgeon will discuss the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks. Make clear that procedure choice is never one-size-fits-all — the surgeon advises based on weight, health conditions, eating patterns, and the patient's goals.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-74-- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Orient the reader to what they will find next — a plain overview of the four main surgical options so they can walk into their consultation with a sense of what the conversation will cover.
--
