   202	> Loose skin is common after significant weight loss. How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age, your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same funding pathways.
   203	>
   204	> Loose skin can occur after significant weight loss, and the amount varies with factors such as age, genetics, skin elasticity and the amount of weight lost. Some patients discuss body-contouring or skin-removal surgery after their weight has stabilised; funding and eligibility differ from bariatric surgery, so confirm any coverage with your surgeon and insurer.
   205	
   206	> **Compress the time-off-work FAQ answer to two sentences.**
   207	>
   208	> Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace, though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early, particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or extended periods on your feet.
   209	>
   210	> Many people need around 2–3 weeks away from desk-based work, while physical or manual work may require longer. Your surgeon will advise based on your recovery and job demands, especially if your role involves lifting, physical exertion or prolonged periods on your feet.
   211	
   212	> **Compress the “what if it doesn't work” FAQ answer to two sentences.**
   213	>
   214	> A gastric bypass is one of the most effective bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet, habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.
   215	>
   216	> Gastric bypass can be an effective bariatric procedure for suitable patients, but results depend on surgery, follow-up care, nutrition, activity and long-term behaviour change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs, your team can review your diet, symptoms and follow-up plan; further assessment or revision surgery may be considered in selected cases.
   217	
   218	> **Compress the eating FAQ answer to two sentences.**
   454	6. **The FAQ answer starts with promotional phrasing**
   455	
   456	> The great news is that most foods can be reintroduced over time.
   457	
   458	> Most foods can be reintroduced gradually over time, but eating usually feels different after gastric bypass.
   459	
   460	Why it matters: The rewrite is calmer and more appropriate for a medical FAQ.
   461	
   462	7. **The sleeve comparison oversimplifies sleeve anatomy**
